# Retail Profitability Analytics

A data analytics and machine learning project that explores
patterns associated with unprofitable retail orders and presents
the findings through an interactive Power BI dashboard.

**Technologies:** Python, Scikit-learn, Power BI

## Overview

High sales do not always mean high profits. Retail businesses
can lose money on individual orders because of discounting,
product-level performance and other operational factors.

This project analyses a synthetic dataset containing
**105,000 retail records** to identify profitability patterns,
investigate loss-making orders and communicate useful insights.

It combines data analysis, a Gradient Boosting classification
model and an interactive Power BI dashboard.

> This is a simulated business case. The results do not represent
> financial outcomes achieved for a real client.

## Objectives

- Identify patterns associated with loss-making orders.
- Examine the relationship between discounts and profitability.
- Identify product areas contributing heavily to losses.
- Build a model that flags potentially unprofitable orders.
- Present the findings through an interactive dashboard.

## Key Findings

| Finding | Result |
|---|---:|
| Records analysed | 105,000 |
| Loss-making orders in the above-20% discount group | 59.3% |
| Loss-making orders in the comparison group | 19.7% |
| Loss identified across two product sub-categories | $647K |

These findings describe patterns within the synthetic dataset.
They should not be interpreted as real-world financial savings
or proof that discounting alone caused the losses.

## Analytical Components

### 1. Discount and Profitability Analysis

Compared the share of loss-making orders across discount groups
to understand how discount levels are associated with profitability.

### 2. Product-Level Loss Analysis

Investigated losses across product sub-categories and identified
two sub-categories accounting for $647K in losses within the
simulated dataset.

### 3. Machine Learning

Built a **Gradient Boosting classifier** using Scikit-learn
to flag unprofitable orders.

The classification component complements the descriptive
analysis by identifying patterns associated with profitability.

